# #4471 contains two problems, not one

The desk merged 412 reports about incorrect invitation dates into #4471. After removing repeated export rows and classifying each report from its tenant locale, booking form, and channel, those reports split into two causes.

| Cause | Reports | Tenants | Seen |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Shared parser reads day-first input as month-first | 379 | 11 | 2026-04-01 to 2026-10-04 |
| `booking-v3` shows a month-first hint to day-first tenants | 33 | 3 | 2026-04-17 to 2026-09-22 |

## Shared parser: 379 reports

The common parser treats every numeric date as `MM/DD/YYYY`. A tenant that writes `DD/MM/YYYY` therefore has its day and month exchanged whenever the values can both be read as months. Intake labelled 356 of these reports as `parser` and 23 as `hint`; intake labels are guesses, so the tenant configuration determines the cause.

Five reports were read by the desk as a one-day move. Their tenant locale and booking form place them in this parser cause, so they remain counted here.

## booking-v3 hint: 33 reports

`booking-v3` prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field for day-first tenants. People follow that instruction, but the form interprets their input with the tenant's day-first order. Intake labelled 18 of these reports as `hint` and 15 as `parser`.

This is a form-contract defect, not the common parser defect. It needs a separate fix and a separate ticket.

## Counting method

The export repeats two report IDs because ticket-system pages overlap. Counts above use one record per report ID, then classify a report from the tenant directory: a day-first tenant's `form` report through `booking-v3` belongs to the hint cause; every other report from a day-first tenant belongs to the parser cause.

